          {
            "alt": "An image shows four sets of medical pictures. The first is the torso and arms of a human skeleton with purple shading in the muscular regions. The second is a set of four brain scans: the top left shows an oval-shaped image that is blue on the outer edge, green as you move inward, and bright red near the center. The top right shows an oval-shaped image that is shaded bright green over most of the image and bright red near the center. The lower left of the four brain scans shows an oval-shaped image that is almost entirely blue with two small, yellow-rimmed red dots near the upper middle section. The lower right image looks very similar to the lower left, but the red regions are slightly larger. The final image shows two scans of a human torso that is turned to face to the side. The left scan has three bright yellow-white areas: one in the throat, one in the chest, and one in the head. The right scan is the same as the left except the bright regions are dim and the internal organs are more clearly defined.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Nuclear chemistry provides the basis for many practical diagnostic and therapeutic medical methods, such as positron emission tomography (PET) scans. The PET/computed tomography scan on the left shows muscle activity. The scans in the center compare chemical differences in dopamine signaling in various brains. The images on the right show an oncological application of PET scans to identify lymph node metastasis."
                  }
                ],
