      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The chemical reactions we have considered in previous units involve changes in the electronic structure of the species involved, that is, the arrangement of the electrons around atoms, ions, or molecules. The nuclear structure remains unchanged during chemical reactions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3516738342",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "This unit will introduce nuclear chemistry, which began with the discovery of radioactivity in 1896 by French physicist Antoine Becquerel.  Nuclear reactions involve changes in the nuclear structure of atoms, altering the number of protons and neutrons within the nucleus. This can lead to the formation of new elements or isotopes and often results in the release or absorption of significant amounts of energy. Throughout the 20th and 21st centuries, nuclear chemistry has become essential for advancements in energy, medicine, geology, and numerous other fields."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2389782087",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
